  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provide comprehensive WASH, Shelter/NFI, and Protection interventions to mitigate morbidity and mortality among children and their families in response to the Sudan crisis</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtltbrgtHumanitarian needs in Sudan have escalated due to ongoing conflict, resulting in severe human casualties, food insecurity, displacement, and damage to essential infrastructure like water and healthcare. The situation has deteriorated even in previously unaffected areas, leaving 24.8 million people in need of humanitarian assistance in 2024, including 48% who are children. In response, Save the Children International (SCI), alongside 12 humanitarian partners (50% national), will lead three consortiums to address these urgent needs:ltbrgtWASH Consortium (SCI, LM International, SOS Sahil, Solidarit&#xe9;s, WR): This initiative aims to improve access to safe water and sanitation for about 317,193 individuals. It includes:ltbrgtEmergency Envelope: A rapid response capability for urgent WASH needs, including water trucking, hygiene kit distribution, water quality monitoring, waste management, and constructing emergency latrines.ltbrgtRecovery Activities: Rehabilitation of water yards and hand pumps, integrating solar systems for sustainable water supply.ltbrgtInstitutional WASH Activities: Targeting emergencies like cholera outbreaks, ensuring health, nutrition, and educational facilities are equipped with necessary WASH infrastructure.ltbrgtShelter and NFIs Consortium (SCI, ACTED, UPO, SOS Sahel): Aims to assist 78,440 vulnerable internally displaced persons and host communities through:ltbrgtCash for Shelter for vulnerable households.ltbrgtDistribution of NFI kits to 4,400 IDP households and 840 vulnerable host community households.ltbrgtTraining on emergency shelter construction and NFI distribution processes.ltbrgtProtection Consortium (SCI, NADA, Nonviolent Peaceforce, CDF, CAFA, DRC, JASMAR): Aims to support over 206,000 people by addressing protection risks for conflict-affected boys and girls through:ltbrgtStrengthening community-based structures, awareness campaigns, and providing mental health support.ltbrgtFocus on gender-based violence (GBV) prevention and response via case management and capacity building for caseworkers.ltbrgtAdditionally, DRC and JASMAR will continue efforts to clear explosive remnants of war from civilian areas, enhancing community safety through risk education. This includes using social media for rapid information dissemination, particularly targeting young men and boys who are most at risk.These consortiums will function under a unified governance structure, leveraging combined expertise to deliver effective interventions to the most affected communities in Sudanltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
